{"task": {"id": "I_kwDOUFmNcc8AAAABUZLLMg", "item_id": "PVTI_lAHOAHSLSM4BlSCUzg967hs", "project_id": "PVT_kwHOAHSLSM4BlSCU", "number": 34, "title": "Improve demo landing page with a Framer Gallery-inspired visual showcase", "body": "# Goal

Redesign the top-level demo/showcase landing page so the games themselves are visually compelling before the user clicks into them.

Use **Framer Gallery** as a visual reference for the overall presentation style: large media-first cards, generous whitespace, minimal copy, restrained chrome, and a polished gallery feel.

This should be an original implementation for `three-game-kit`, not a visual copy of Framer.

## Current problem

The current top page is functional but visually weak:

- every showcase is presented as a similar text-heavy card;
- there are no representative game images;
- the individual visual identity of Craftlands, Relic Frontier, Afterglow, Gravetide, Deepfield, etc. is largely hidden;
- long descriptions compete with the actual playable content;
- the page feels more like a documentation index than a gallery of finished browser games.

The repository now has enough visually distinct demos that the landing page should communicate:

> "These are real playable 3D games built with three-game-kit."

## Design direction

Follow these principles:

### Media first

Each showcase card should be dominated by a representative image or captured game frame.

Target roughly:

```text
70–80% visual
20–30% title / metadata
```

The screenshot should communicate the genre before the user reads the text.

### Minimal copy

Prefer:

```text
Craftlands
Survival Sandbox
```

over a full paragraph on the gallery page.

Detailed descriptions remain in each showcase itself and in repository documentation.

### Generous spacing

Use a clean, editorial gallery layout:

- wide content area;
- generous vertical rhythm;
- clear grouping;
- restrained borders/shadows;
- fewer visible UI decorations;
- typography and spacing should do most of the work.

### Strong hierarchy

Consider:

1. simple page header;
2. optional featured showcase;
3. primary showcase gallery;
4. smaller "Feature Lab / Experiments" section for technical demos such as Particle Atlas;
5. unobtrusive GitHub/source footer.

Do not let `Core Run` or technical examples visually compete with the strongest polished games unless the composition benefits from it.

## Suggested information architecture

Example direction only:

```text
three-game-kit                         GitHub ↗

PLAYABLE SHOWCASES
Small 3D games built from public three-game-kit Features.

[ large featured visual ]

Craftlands
Survival Sandbox                          Play →

EXPLORE

[ Relic Frontier ]  [ Afterglow      ]
[ Gravetide       ]  [ Deepfield      ]
[ Chroma Strike   ]  [ ...            ]

FEATURE LAB

[ Particle Atlas ]

Built with Three.js · View source
```

The exact ordering should be chosen based on visual quality rather than commit age.

## Showcase cards

At minimum each primary card should support:

- representative screenshot/cover image;
- showcase title;
- short genre/category label;
- whole-card navigation to the playable demo;
- clear hover/focus state;
- keyboard accessibility;
- responsive behavior.

Optional metadata, only if it improves the design:

- Touch;
- Gamepad;
- First-person / Third-person;
- Voxel;
- Survival;
- Racing;
- Action.

Avoid turning the card into a dense tag cloud.

## Screenshots / cover images

Add a deliberate cover image for each major showcase.

At minimum cover:

- Craftlands;
- Relic Frontier;
- Chroma Strike;
- Gravetide;
- Afterglow;
- Deepfield;
- Core Run;
- Particle Atlas.

Prefer stable authored/captured images checked into the repository over relying on a live canvas as the initial gallery thumbnail.

Requirements:

- consistent aspect ratio;
- reasonable compressed file size;
- documented provenance;
- no external hotlinked assets;
- no layout shift while loading;
- sensible responsive image sizing.

If appropriate, add a repeatable screenshot-capture workflow using each showcase's deterministic QA mode so covers can be regenerated.

## Interaction

Keep interaction subtle.

Good candidates:

- slight image scale on hover;
- small title/metadata movement;
- restrained reveal of `Play` or an arrow;
- smooth focus treatment.

Avoid excessive parallax, tilt, glow, animated gradients, or effects that make the gallery feel like a template demo.

Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.

## Responsive design

The page should feel intentional across:

- desktop;
- tablet;
- phone.

On narrow screens:

- one-column cards are acceptable;
- screenshots remain prominent;
- touch targets must be comfortable;
- hover-only information must not be required.

## Technical constraints

- Keep the page lightweight.
- No frontend framework should be introduced solely for this page unless clearly justified.
- Continue to work with the existing Vite / GitHub Pages multi-page build.
- Keep all links compatible with the repository Pages base path.
- Do not introduce remote runtime dependencies for the landing page.
- Maintain semantic HTML and keyboard navigation.
- Avoid compromising showcase startup/performance merely to decorate the index.

## Visual reference

Primary reference:

- Framer Gallery: https://www.framer.com/community/gallery/

Borrow the **design principles**, not exact components, dimensions, typography, imagery, or branding:

- visual-first cards;
- minimal text;
- generous whitespace;
- polished editorial grid;
- quiet navigation and metadata.

The resulting page should still feel like `three-game-kit`: technical, modern, playful, and focused on playable Web3D games.

## Acceptance criteria

- [ ] Landing page is substantially redesigned from the current text-card grid.
- [ ] Major playable showcases use representative cover images.
- [ ] Game visuals are the dominant element of the page.
- [ ] Card copy is reduced to concise title/category information.
- [ ] Layout has clear visual hierarchy and generous whitespace.
- [ ] Desktop/tablet/mobile layouts are intentionally designed.
- [ ] Hover, keyboard focus, and touch interactions are usable.
- [ ] Reduced-motion preference is respected.
- [ ] All existing showcase links still work in local development and GitHub Pages.
- [ ] No remote assets or third-party branding are copied from Framer.
- [ ] Cover assets have provenance and reasonable file sizes.
- [ ] The page still builds through `pnpm run build:pages`.
- [ ] Add automated checks for broken showcase links / required covers where practical.
- [ ] Final result makes the page feel like a curated playable game gallery rather than a documentation index.
